---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const home = base || '/';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Texture Generation with Neural Cellular Automata | Baptiste Lefebvre"
  description="An implementation and experimental study of neural cellular automata for texture generation, including VGG perceptual losses, multi-texture generation and Sliced Optimal Transport."
>
  <header class="nav">
    <a class="wordmark" href={home} aria-label="Home">Baptiste Lefebvre<span>.</span></a>
    <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
      <a href={`${home}#projects`}>Projects</a>
      <a href={`${home}#about`}>About</a>
      <a href={`${home}#contact`}>Contact</a>
    </nav>
    <a class="nav-link" href="https://github.com/baptlef" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub <span>↗</span></a>
  </header>

  <main>
    <a class="back-link" href={`${home}#projects`}>← Back to all projects</a>

    <section class="project-hero">
      <div class="eyebrow"><span class="availability-dot"></span>GENERATIVE MODELS · PROJECT</div>
      <h1>Texture Generation with <span>Neural Cellular Automata.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Can a learned local update rule grow a convincing texture from noise?
        This 4-person team project project implements a neural cellular automaton from scratch and
        investigates perceptual texture losses, multi-texture synthesis and
        Sliced Optimal Transport.
      </p>
      
      <div class="meta-row">
        <span class="meta-label">REFERENCE:</span>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.07299" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="paper-badge">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2H20v20H6.5a2.5 2.5 0 0 1-2.5-2.5Z"/><path d="M6 6h10"/><path d="M6 10h10"/></svg>
          <span>Mordvintsev et al. — <em>Texture Generation with Neural Cellular Automata</em> (2021)</span>
          <span class="arxiv-tag">arXiv:2105.07299</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
      </div>

      <figure class="hero-figure">
        <img src={`${base}/images/nca/nca-hero.png`} alt="Four examples of textures generated by the neural cellular automaton" />
        <figcaption>Four examples of textures generated by the neural cellular automaton.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="content-grid">
      <aside class="side-label">
        <span>01 / OVERVIEW</span>
        <span>PROJECT NOTES</span>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Growing texture through local rules</h2>
        <p>
          A neural cellular automaton (NCA) represents an image as a grid of cells.
          Each cell stores a state vector and updates that state using information
          from its local neighborhood. Repeating the learned update produces
          large-scale texture from an initial seed.
        </p>
        <p>
          The implementation was built from scratch, following the project’s
          reference paper, <em>Texture Generation with Neural Cellular Automata</em>.
          The main challenge was to train a shared local rule whose repeated
          application produces texture statistics resembling a target image.
        </p>
        <div class="callout">
          <span class="callout-label">CORE IDEA</span>
          <p>Rather than directly generating every pixel, learn a local dynamical system whose repeated updates produce the desired visual pattern.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-grid">
      <aside class="side-label">
        <span>02 / METHOD</span>
        <span>TRAINING LOOP</span>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Training the automaton</h2>
        <p>
          Training alternates between simulating the automaton and comparing
          the resulting image with the target texture using a perceptual loss
          based on a pretrained VGG network. Gradients are backpropagated through
          the unrolled simulation to update the NCA parameters.
        </p>
        <div class="method-steps">
          <div class="method-step"><span>01</span><div><strong>Sample states from a pool</strong><p>A batch of four states is sampled from the training pool.</p></div></div>
          <div class="method-step"><span>02</span><div><strong>Simulate local updates</strong><p>Each cell computes a perception vector from its neighborhood and updates its state stochastically.</p></div></div>
          <div class="method-step"><span>03</span><div><strong>Measure perceptual similarity</strong><p>VGG features are compared with the target texture. The initial loss uses Gram matrices to capture feature correlations.</p></div></div>
          <div class="method-step"><span>04</span><div><strong>Backpropagate and refresh the pool</strong><p>Adam updates the parameters, and the evolved states are re-injected into the pool.</p></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-grid">
          <div class="stat"><strong>5,868</strong><span>parameters in the reported model</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><strong>4</strong><span>states sampled per training batch</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><strong>10,000</strong><span>training steps</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="small-note">We use VGG feature layers 1, 6, 11 and 18 for the Gram-matrix texture loss.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-grid">
      <aside class="side-label">
        <span>03 / EXPERIMENTS</span>
        <span>TRAINING DURATION</span>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>More updates help — but do not solve everything</h2>
        <p>
          We compare outputs after 5,000 and 10,000 training
          steps. For complex textures such as leopard-like fur, the longer
          training run captured more of the fine detail and spots.
        </p>
        <figure class="result-figure">
          <img src={`${base}/images/nca/nca-training-comparison.png`} alt="Comparison of source textures and NCA outputs at 5,000 and 10,000 training steps for several texture types" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Qualitative comparison, from left to right: source texture, 5,000 training steps and 10,000 training steps.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="insight-grid">
          <div><span class="mini-label">OBSERVATION</span><h3>Training duration matters</h3><p>Increasing training from 5,000 to 10,000 steps visibly improved some complex patterns.</p></div>
          <div><span class="mini-label">LIMITATION</span><h3>Locality can limit global structure</h3><p>Textures requiring long-range spatial coherence were difficult to reproduce with purely local processing.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-grid">
      <aside class="side-label">
        <span>04 / EXTENSION</span>
        <span>MULTI-TEXTURE</span>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>One model, multiple textures</h2>
        <p>
          The multi-texture extension adds genomic channels to the cell state.
          A binary texture identifier conditions the cellular automaton, allowing
          the same update rule to generate different target textures. The
          presentation shows four target textures trained jointly over 40,000
          iterations.
        </p>
        <figure class="result-figure">
          <img src={`${base}/images/nca/nca-multi-texture.png`} alt="Four target textures and corresponding generated textures for the multi-texture NCA experiment" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Four target textures and corresponding generated textures for the multi-texture NCA experiment.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-grid">
      <aside class="side-label">
        <span>05 / LOSS DESIGN</span>
        <span>SLICED OT</span>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Replacing Gram statistics with Sliced Optimal Transport</h2>
        <p>
          Another of our experiment applies Sliced Optimal Transport (SOT) to VGG
          features instead of comparing Gram matrices, while also incorporating
          RGB information. The aim is to compare feature distributions more
          directly rather than relying only on their channel-correlation
          statistics.
        </p>
        <figure class="result-figure">
          <img src={`${base}/images/nca/nca-sliced-optimal-transport.png`} alt="Target dotted texture and the corresponding NCA result in the Sliced Optimal Transport experiment" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Target and generated results with Gram-based texture loss and SOT. Despite improvements in color fidelity with SOT, this texture remained challenging.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="callout">
          <span class="callout-label">TAKEAWAY</span>
          <p>SOT was a useful alternative to investigate, but it did not remove all difficulties with textures containing varied colors and long-range spatial coherence.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-grid">
      <aside class="side-label">
        <span>06 / ROBUSTNESS</span>
        <span>REGENERATION</span>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Can the texture recover after damage?</h2>
        <p>
          Our project also tests regeneration after a circular region is damaged. Applying the learned update rule, the local dynamics progressively restore the
          texture pattern.
        </p>
        <figure class="result-figure">
          <img src={`${base}/images/nca/nca-regeneration.png`} alt="Sequence showing a bubble texture recovering after circular damage over repeated NCA updates" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Regeneration after circular damage: the sequence illustrates the texture returning toward its original pattern over subsequent updates.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-grid">
      <aside class="side-label">
        <span>07 / CONCLUSIONS</span>
        <span>WHAT I LEARNED</span>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What the experiments revealed</h2>
        <ul class="takeaways">
          <li><strong>Perceptual objectives shape the result.</strong> VGG features provide a way to compare texture appearance beyond raw pixel-wise differences.</li>
          <li><strong>Local rules have characteristic failure modes.</strong> Patterns that need global spatial organization are harder to model with local updates alone.</li>
          <li><strong>More training is not a universal fix.</strong> Longer training helped some textures, but the dotted texture still produced color and structure mismatches.</li>
          <li><strong>Conditioning enables reuse.</strong> Adding genomic channels and a texture identifier made multi-texture generation possible within one model.</li>
          <li><strong>Self-repair is a compelling NCA property.</strong> The damage-reconstruction experiment demonstrates how a learned dynamical system can restore a familiar pattern.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="next-box">
          <span class="callout-label">PROJECT RESOURCES</span>
          <p>
            Want to know more?
            Check out our github repository for the code, training scripts and additional results.
          </p>
          <a href="https://github.com/helo-grn/Synth-se-de-textures-par-automates-cellulaires" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="button button-dark">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M15 22v-4a4.8 4.8 0 0 0-1-3.5c3 0 6-2 6-5.5.08-1.25-.27-2.48-1-3.5.28-1.15.28-2.35 0-3.5 0 0-1 0-3 1.5-2.64-.5-5.36-.5-8 0C6 2 5 2 5 2c-.3 1.15-.3 2.35 0 3.5A5.403 5.403 0 0 0 4 9c0 3.5 3 5.5 6 5.5-.39.49-.68 1.05-.85 1.65-.17.6-.22 1.23-.15 1.85v4"/><path d="M9 18c-4.51 2-5-2-7-2"/></svg>
            GitHub Repo 
            <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .nav{max-width:1180px;margin:0 auto;padding:25px 32px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}
  .wordmark{font:800 25px 'Manrope',sans-serif;letter-spacing:-2px}.wordmark span{color:var(--green)}
  nav{display:flex;gap:32px}nav a,.nav-link{color:#656961;font-size:13px}nav a:hover,.nav-link:hover{color:var(--green)}.nav-link{color:#252923}
  main{max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 32px}.back-link{display:inline-block;margin:30px 0;color:#657064;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;transition:color 0.2s;}.back-link:hover{color:var(--green)}
  .eyebrow{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:9px;color:var(--green);font-size:10px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1.6px}.availability-dot{width:7px;height:7px;border-radius:50%;background:#6e9b73;box-shadow:0 0 0 4px #e8eee6}
  .project-hero{padding:35px 0 70px}.project-hero h1{max-width:850px;margin:22px 0;font:600 clamp(38px,5.6vw,64px)/1.1 'Manrope',sans-serif;letter-spacing:-2.6px}.project-hero h1 span{color:#83887e}.lead{max-width:740px;color:var(--muted);font-size:16px;line-height:1.9}
  
  .meta-row{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;margin:22px 0 32px;font-size:12px;}
  .meta-label{color:#85897f;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;text-transform:uppercase;} 
  .paper-badge{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;padding:6px 10px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:4px;background:#ffffff;color:#454942;text-decoration:none;font-size:12px;transition:transform 0.2s, border-color 0.2s, color 0.2s;}
  .paper-badge:hover{transform:translateY(-1px);border-color:#b0b8a8;color:var(--green);}
  .paper-badge svg{color:#798375;flex-shrink:0;}
  .arxiv-tag{padding:2px 6px;border-radius:3px;background:#f0f2eb;color:#555d52;font-size:10px;font-family:monospace;}

  figure{margin:0}.hero-figure{overflow:hidden;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:5px;background:#f0f1e9}.hero-figure img{display:block;width:100%;max-height:510px;object-fit:contain}.hero-figure figcaption,.result-figure figcaption{padding:12px 16px;color:#777d72;background:#f8f8f4;font-size:10px;line-height:1.7}
  .content-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:175px minmax(0,1fr);gap:55px;padding:54px 0;border-top:1px solid var(--line)}.side-label{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px;color:#788073;font-size:9px;letter-spacing:1.1px}.side-label span:first-child{color:var(--green);font-weight:700}
  .prose{min-width:0}.prose h2{margin:0 0 20px;font:600 clamp(27px,3vw,36px)/1.2 'Manrope',sans-serif;letter-spacing:-1.3px}.prose>p{color:#646b61;font-size:13px;line-height:1.95}.prose em{color:#30372f}.callout{margin:26px 0;padding:20px 22px;border-left:3px solid #75927a;background:#f0f2eb}.callout-label,.mini-label{color:var(--green);font-size:9px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1.3px}.callout p{margin:10px 0 0;color:#525b50;font-size:13px;line-height:1.8}
  .method-steps{margin:28px 0}.method-step{display:grid;grid-template-columns:42px 1fr;gap:14px;padding:18px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}.method-step>span{color:#81907b;font:600 18px 'Manrope',sans-serif}.method-step strong{font-size:13px}.method-step p{margin:6px 0 0;color:var(--muted);font-size:12px;line-height:1.8}
  .stat-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:12px;margin:28px 0}.stat{padding:19px 15px;border:1px solid var(--line);background:#fff}.stat strong{display:block;font:700 22px 'Manrope',sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.8px}.stat span{display:block;margin-top:7px;color:var(--muted);font-size:10px;line-height:1.6}.small-note{font-size:11px!important}
  .result-figure{margin:28px 0;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:5px;overflow:hidden;background:#f5f6f1}.result-figure img{display:block;width:100%;max-height:530px;object-fit:contain}.insight-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:15px;margin-top:24px}.insight-grid>div{padding:20px;border:1px solid var(--line);background:#fff}.insight-grid h3{margin:10px 0;font:600 16px 'Manrope',sans-serif}.insight-grid p{margin:0;color:var(--muted);font-size:12px;line-height:1.8}
  .takeaways{padding-left:20px}.takeaways li{margin:14px 0;padding-left:6px;color:#646b61;font-size:13px;line-height:1.85}.takeaways strong{color:#30372f}.next-box{margin-top:30px;padding:23px;border:1px solid var(--line);background:#f7f8f3}.next-box p{margin:10px 0 20px;color:var(--muted);font-size:12px;line-height:1.8}
  .button{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:18px;padding:13px 17px;border:1px solid transparent;border-radius:5px;font-size:12px;font-weight:600;text-decoration:none;}.button-dark{color:white;background:#26382d}.button-dark:hover{background:#3d644c}
  
  footer{max-width:1180px;margin:0 auto;padding:27px 32px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:20px;color:#83877e;font-size:10px}footer .wordmark{color:##20251f}
  @media(max-width:700px){.nav{padding:20px}nav{gap:15px}nav a{font-size:11px}.nav-link{display:none}main{padding:0 20px}.project-hero{padding-top:25px}.project-hero h1{font-size:42px}.content-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:20px;padding:38px 0}.side-label{flex-direction:row;justify-content:space-between}.stat-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.insight-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.hero-figure img{min-height:180px;object-fit:cover}footer{padding:25px 20px;flex-wrap:wrap}}
</style>